<td id="A4.T13.6.1.10.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">—</td>
<td id="A4.T13.6.1.10.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.746 (0.003)</td>
<td id="A4.T13.6.1.10.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.783 (0.003)</td>
<td id="A4.T13.6.1.10.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">—</td>
<td id="A4.T13.6.1.10.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">—</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="A4.T14" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 14: </span>Impact of BPE merge count (<math id="A4.T14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>) on compression ratio and model performance (ZINC). <math id="A4.T14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=2000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=2000</annotation></semantics></math> achieves the best balance; larger vocabularies yield diminishing compression returns and do not improve performance due to token rarity.</figcaption>
